@article{GV21,
  title   = {Interacting thermofield doubles and critical behavior in random regular graphs},
  journal = {Phys. Rev. D},
  volume  = {103},
  pages   = {106013},
  year    = {2021},
  eprint  = {2101.04072},
  archivePrefix = {arXiv},
  author  = {Gorsky, A. and Valba, O.},
  note    = {read-full (2026-09-21, by the owner, arXiv version). **Two corrections to what this entry used to say.** (1) It is NOT mean-field: it is Monte Carlo on real graphs. (2) It was marked abstract-only. Only Sec. 2 bears on this project. Energy is the square count alone -- Eq. (2.6) gives R_ij = -(1/d)[(2d-2) - N4(ij)] with no saturation bracket, so this is our lambda = 0 (penalty-off) case; H = mu4 Tr A^4, Eq. (2.3). Hard-core rule as ours (two squares share at most one link). **Bipartiteness is not imposed**: they start from ordinary random regular graphs and bipartiteness EMERGES in the clustered phase (Sec. 2.3), where our kernel imposes it. For degree > 3 they report a **first-order transition with clear hysteresis** (Fig. 3A, d = 4 at several N; Fig. 6, N = 256 across degrees). Cold phase: about N/2^d isolated or weakly interacting d-hypercubes (Eq. 2.11) PLUS one closed bipartite "ribbon" holding the leftover nodes, whose share depends on mu4 (Fig. 3B). Under a connectedness constraint, a closed chain of weakly linked hypercubes (Fig. 7, N = 128), all 4-cycles in hypercubes. Degree 3 gives a single closed ribbon, agreeing with KTB20 (unread). Sizes N = 128, 256; degrees 3-8. **They criticise the Trugenberger group's earlier runs as too small to hold more than one hypercube**, so the configuration looked homogeneous -- the same finite-size argument this project makes, in print since 2021 and for the penalty-off energy only. Secs. 3 and 4 (thermofield doubles as eternal black holes; Hilbert-space fragmentation) are the authors' conjectures and are not testable with our code. **To verify before comparing any number:** coupling units (on a d-regular graph Tr A^4 = 8 x squares + const, so mu4 maps to 8 mu4 per square; ours, unverified) and the sign convention of Eq. (2.1), which writes exp(-mu4 N4) while the text maximises 4-cycles -- read the axis as "larger mu4 favours more squares" and confirm against Fig. 3A. Their Metropolis uses degree-preserving link swaps (citing Viger-Latapy); the connectivity-preserving variant is not detailed.}
}

