@phdthesis{Kelly22,
  author  = {Kelly, Christy},
  title   = {Emergent geometry and discrete curvature in random graphs},
  school  = {Heriot-Watt University},
  year    = {2022},
  month   = {April},
  url     = {https://www.ros.hw.ac.uk/handle/10399/4591},
  note    = {**ABSTRACT READ DIRECTLY (2026-09-21); full text NOT read -- the repository PDF link 404s and must be obtained another way. Read it before this entry is relied on for anything.** By the first author of KTB19. Three statements from the abstract, quoted exactly. (1) For cubic graphs: "numerical evidence suggests that the transition to this geometric phase is continuous". (2) **"Higher degree results are less conclusive and the phase transition appears to become first-order, but there are good analytical and numerical indications that configurations retain a nearly geometric phase."** (3) "The critical temperature appears to be asymptotically nonfinite". **Why this matters more than anything else found so far.** Sentence (2) is the combination VISION claim 4 needs -- first order AND a space -- stated by the group's own first author, for degrees above 3, which includes the degree-4 case this project simulates. It sits against [T25], which reports the transition as continuous, and beside [GV21], which finds first order for degree > 3 independently. Our own framing that "where it is sharp there is no space and where there is a space it is smooth" is a statement about the ground state at two settings of lambda, and this is evidence that it does not hold for the transition itself. **Caveats, all the author's own or ours:** "less conclusive"; "appears to"; "nearly geometric" is not geometric; we have read one paragraph. Unknown from the abstract: which degrees "higher degree" covers, which variant of the energy, and what sizes. Sentence (3) also bears on T9: a critical temperature that is not asymptotically finite is the same phenomenon as the drift of the crossover with ln N that we measure.}
}

